{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":5,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nematicides (+ \"removed from the market\" withdrawn banned phased-out) (+ health environmental toxicity))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nematicides (+ \"removed from the market\" withdrawn banned phased-out) (+ health environmental toxicity))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1716969794#5_3210664013","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1479","rank":1,"snippet":"Vegetables (including Herbs) Plant Protection Products Occupational exposure to chemicals and reproductive health Male effects Aldicarb Uses Lawn and Turf 47.2.2.3 Nematodes and Nematicides Recommended publications \" , \"segment\": \"In different countries, different ranges of fumigants (e.g. methyl bromide and dichloropropene), precursors of fumigants (e.g. dazomet) and nonvolatile nematicides (in granular form such as aldicarb) are still authorized for use but the list is becoming more and more restricted because of the negative <cover>environmental impact of the chemicals. Methylbromide, for example, which has a remarkably wide-spectrum activity (including weeds, soil fungi, nematodes and insects), will be banned in all countries of the European Union in 2005. Nematicides </cover>protect young plantlets during the first stages of their development and allow their satisfactory installation and subsequent productivity. None the less, none of the nematicides can eradicate nematodes from the soil because individuals can usually survive in deeper levels where they can escape chemical diffusion; reinfestation of the plants will inevitably occur after a few months or years. It is absolutely necessary that preplanting chemical treatments, which act only against nematode stages surviving in the bare soil, are combined with the use of "},"time":193},"model_calls":5,"document_judgments":2,"relevance_3":2,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
