{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":13,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1482"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1482"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_866237787#0_1763779106","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1482","rank":3,"snippet":"\" , \"end_char\": 1201.000000 , \"headings\": \"Methyl Bromide Methyl Bromide What Is Methyl Bromide? Methyl bromide damages the ozone layer Find more information on methyl bromide: Key Resource The Phaseout of Methyl Bromide Methyl Bromide Alternatives Learn more: Methyl Bromide Critical Uses Methyl Bromide for Quarantine and Preshipment Quarantine Applications Preshipment Applications Apply for a Methyl Bromide Critical Use Exemption Pre-plant application form Post-harvest application form Methyl Bromide Reporting Forms \" , \"segment\": \"Methyl Bromide | <cover>Phaseout of Ozone-Depleting Substances (ODS) | US EPA Methyl Bromide Methyl bromide is a fumigant </cover>used to control pests in agriculture and shipping. Methyl bromide also depletes the ozone layer. Therefore, along with other countries, the United States has phased out production and consumption of methyl bromide with important exceptions for critical uses as well as quarantine and preshipment. Learn more about protecting the ozone layer. Overview Alternatives Critical Uses Quarantine and Preshipment Forms What Is Methyl Bromide? Methyl bromide is an odorless, colorless gas used to control a wide variety of pests in agriculture and shipping, including fungi, weeds, insects, nematodes (or roundworms), and rodents. Agricultural growers ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_96948120#9_110518303","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_96948120#10_110520620","rank":2,"relevance":2}]},"time":8},"model_calls":13,"document_judgments":10,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
