{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":48,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1508"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1508"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_270229525#10_485580861","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1508","rank":2,"snippet":": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_270229525#10_485580861\" , \"end_char\": 8279.000000 , \"headings\": \"Chemical control of nematodes: efficiency and side-effects Chemical control of nematodes: efficiency and side-effects S.R. Gowen Introduction<cover>Chemical control of nematodes The demand Efficacy TABLE 1 Nematicides currently available on world markets TABLE 2 Examples of recommended nematicidal dosages and treatments for some important crops1 Side-effects Future developments References \" , \"segment\": \"Compounds releasing methyl isothiocyanate (dazomet, metam-sodium) work best in soils at >15°C. In cooler soils, the period between treatment and planting may have to be extended to allow sufficient time for the product to disperse. The liquid fumigant DBCP is the only volatile compound that can be applied to growing plants without causing phytotoxicity. However, its manufacture has now ceased for toxicological reasons </cover>and its use is banned in many countries. Non-volatile nematicides. A number of organophosphate and oximecarbamate nematicides were developed in the 1960s, which had the advantage that application was relatively simple (Wright, 1981). As a consequence nematicide use became more widely practised. These compounds (Table 1) ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_270229525#9_485579305","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":48,"document_judgments":27,"relevance_3":14,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
