{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":64,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1523"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1523"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_614024996#15_563342684","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1523","rank":4,"snippet":"a large percentage of Nanidorus minor populations occurred between 8 to 16  inches deep, below the typical treatment zone of soil fumigants. This allows many Nanidorus minor to  escape being killed by fumigant treatments. Population numbers of this nematode are known to  rebound following soil fumigation to numbers higher than if no treatment were used. Therefore,  soil fumigants, while effective treatments for other plant-parasitic nematodes in Florida, often are  not recommended for management of Nanidorus minor . Continuous cultivation of highly susceptible crops such as corn or sorghum can build up  populations of Nanidorus minor to damaging numbers and may require the use of nematicides on  subsequent crops. Summer legumes such as velvetbean or cowpea tend to keep populations of Nanidorus minor low and may reduce reliance on nematicides. In the past, organophosphate and carbamate nematicides such as aldicarb and fenamiphos were very effective for management of Nanidorus minor. However, these <cover>nematicides have been withdrawn due to environmental and health concerns.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 6419.000000 , \"title\": \"stubby-root nematode\" , \"url\": \"http://entnemdept.ufl.edu/creatures/nematode/stubbyroot/paratrichodorus_minor.htm\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_614024996#15_563342684","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":64,"document_judgments":32,"relevance_3":15,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
