{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":80,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"plant-parasitic nematodes\" \"plant parasitic nematodes\") (+ \"chemical nematicides\" nematicides) (+ \"human health\" environmental toxicity risks))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"plant-parasitic nematodes\" \"plant parasitic nematodes\") (+ \"chemical nematicides\" nematicides) (+ \"human health\" environmental toxicity risks))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1179094178#12_2116808331","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1536","rank":1,"snippet":"2116808331\" , \"end_char\": 8415.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"Symptoms include yellowing, wilting, browning, thinning out, or death. Often weeds such as spurge and sedges become prominent as the grass thins out . Nematode damage usually occurs in irregularly shaped patches that may enlarge slowly over time. Be aware that similar conditions may be caused by other factors such as localized soil conditions, fungi, or insects. Because <cover>plant-parasitic nematodes live in soil or roots, the pesticides used to kill them tend to be very toxic and are water soluble. Because of health risks and environmental concerns there are currently no toxic nematicides </cover>that are labeled for use on established home lawns. There are a number of products available for use on home lawns that are marketed as \"organic\", \"biological\" or \"non-toxic\" that claim to be suppressive to plant parasitic nematodes. Be aware that in order to be labeled for home use these types of products need to be safe, but do not need to be proven effective. Nernatologists at numerous universities have tested many of these products for efficacy, generally with disappointing results. "},"time":78},"model_calls":80,"document_judgments":34,"relevance_3":15,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
