{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":82,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1797328243#10_3591147408","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1536","rank":3,"snippet":"\"end_char\": 4168.000000 , \"headings\": \"Nematode Management in the Vegetable Garden 1 Nematode Management in the Vegetable Garden 1 What are nematodes? How do nematodes damage plants? Root-Knot Nematodes Ectoparasites Other Nematodes How do I know if nematodes are a problem? Handling Submitting a Sample What can I do about nematodes? Chemical <cover>Nematicides Bionematicides Resistance and Tolerance Soil Solarization Cover Crops Organic Amendments Planting Soil Tillage and Root Destruction Container Growing Summary Tables \" , \"segment\": \"Panel D, Theresa Friday UF/IFAS Extension How do nematodes damage plants? As plant-parasitic nematodes feed, they damage the root system and reduce the ability of a plant to obtain water and nutrients from the soil. When nematode numbers get high, and/or when environmental </cover>stresses occur, aboveground symptoms may become evident. Aboveground nematode symptoms often resemble nutrient deficiencies or drought stress. Symptoms include yellowing, wilting, and stunting (Figure 7, 8, 9). Figure 7. Nematode-damaged corn is stunted, yellow, and wilting. Damage occurs in patches. Credit: D. W. Dickson, UF/IFAS Figure 8.\" , \"start_char\": 3552.000000 , \"title","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1797328243#10_3591147408","rank":3,"relevance":1}]},"time":17},"model_calls":82,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":82,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"model_calls":82,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":82,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_123562181#15_253441264","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1536","rank":4,"snippet":"parasitic nematodes. However, neuropeptides, unlike the small molecule ‘classical’ transmitters, (e.g., acetylcholine), have diverged considerably in structure during evolution and this presents the promise that drugs targeting peptidergic signalling in nematodes may have low mammalian <cover>toxicity. There has thus been considerable interest in the prospect of developing peptidomimetics as a novel approach for nematode control. A comparative analysis of the neuropeptide content for a range of nematodes has been comprehensively reviewed ( Marks and Maule, 2010; McVeigh et al., 2005) and may become an important consideration if this strategy is to be pursued. Progress has been made towards pairing the neuropeptide ligands with their cognate receptors in C. elegans ( Frooninckx et al., 2012) and this may point to new molecular targets for nematode control. A comparative analysis of the physiology and pharmacology of C. elegans with plant parasitic nematodes is more difficult as there is very little information available for these species in this regard.\" , \"start_char\": 8232.000000 , \"title\": \"Anthelmintic drugs and nematicides: </cover>studies in Caenorhabditis elegans - WormBook - NCBI Bookshelf\" , \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK116072/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1797328243#10_3591147408","rank":3,"relevance":1}]},"time":10},"model_calls":82,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
