{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":83,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"model_calls":83,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":83,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1536"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_508868484#0_571839510","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1536","rank":9,"snippet":": \"Nematicide Nematicide See also References \" , \"segment\": \"Nematicide - Wikipedia Nematicide From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to navigation Jump to search Not to be confused with Nematicon. A nematicide is a type of chemical pesticide used to kill <cover>plant- parasitic nematodes. Nematicides have tended to be broad-spectrum toxicants possessing high volatility or other properties promoting migration through the soil. Aldicarb (Temik), a carbamate insecticide marketed by Bayer CropScience, is an example of a commonly used commercial nematicide. It is important in potato production, where it has been used for control of soil-borne nematodes. Aldicarb is a cholinesterase inhibitor, which prevents the breakdown of acetylcholine in the synapse. In case of severe poisoning, the victim dies of respiratory failure. It is no longer authorised for use in the EU and, in August 2010, Bayer CropScience announced that it planned to discontinue aldicarb by 2014. Human health </cover>safety and environmental concerns have resulted in the widespread deregistration of several other agronomically important nematicides. Prior to 1985, the persistent halocarbon DBCP was a widely used nematicide and soil fumigant.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_508868484#0_571839510","rank":9,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":83,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
