{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-219661","model_call":95,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1546"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1546"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_1062712741#2_1295395898","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1546","rank":2,"snippet":"chloropropane 1,2-Dibromo-3-chloropropane From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Contents Stereoisomerism Uses Sources and potential exposure Examples of persistence Lawsuits Safety References External links \" , \"segment\": \"Infobox references Chemical compound 1,2-Dibromo-3-chloropropane, ( dibromochloropropane) better known as DBCP, is the organic compound with the formula BrCH (CH 2 Br) (CH 2 Cl). It is a dense colorless liquid although commercial samples often appear amber or even brown. It is the active ingredient in the <cover>nematicide Nemagon, also known as Fumazone. It is a soil fumigant formerly used in American agriculture. In mammals it causes male sterility at high levels of exposure. After discovery of its deleterious health </cover>effects on humans, the compound was banned from use in 1979 by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA). The continuing presence of the chemical as a contaminant in ground water remains a problem for many communities for years after end of use. Contents 1 Stereoisomerism 2 Uses 3 Sources and potential exposure 4 Examples of persistence 5 Lawsuits 6 Safety 7 References 8 External links Stereoisomerism 1,2-Dibromo-3-chloropropane (2 stereoisomers) (R)-configuration (S)-configuration Uses Until 1977, DBCP was used ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_1062712741#2_1295395898","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":11},"model_calls":95,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
